72
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:24,840
Lunar touchdown is scheduled for April 15th, four days away.

73
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:32,260
But then, a simple request for equipment maintenance changes everything.

74
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:42,100
Oxygen and hydrogen are kept on board in a supercooled state, minus 340 degrees Fahrenheit.

75
00:05:42,101 --> 00:05:49,600
When mixed in the ship's fuel cells, the gases combine to form electricity, heat, and even drinking water.

76
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,840
Because oxygen and hydrogen in this supercooled state tend to settle unevenly in the tanks,

77
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:02,540
the crew is periodically asked to stir the tanks with a remotely operated electric fan.

78
00:06:02,541 --> 00:06:06,940
Engineers refer to this as a cryo stir.

79
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:16,580
It falls to the Electrical and Environmental Command Officer, or ECOM, to request the procedure.

80
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:23,800
Dan, we've got one more item for you when you get a chance.

81
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:26,460
We'd like it to stir up your cryo tanks.

82
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:29,100
In addition, I have a shaft in front of you.

83
00:06:29,101 --> 00:06:33,340
Just to be a good e-comm, I decided to have an extra stir done before they went to sleep,

84
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:35,700
which was about an hour before the end of my shift.

85
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:38,500
We had a few things we had to clean up before they went to bed,

86
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:43,520
and so I asked the flight director if we could add to the list, how about a stir?

87
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:48,960
Two days, seven hours, and 54 minutes into the mission,

88
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:52,860
Jack Swigert flips the fan switch to stir the tanks.

89
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:58,840
