and could be essentially established as a point source

233
00:18:43,321 --> 00:18:46,500
so that we could use this as the equivalent of a star.

234
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:49,880
As Lovell sights the earth,

235
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:55,100
Hayes sights the moon through a periscope-like device called the Alignment Optical Telescope.

236
00:18:55,880 --> 00:18:59,920
If the alignment is good, the Earth and the moon line up in the sights,

237
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:03,560
verifying that the navigational system is on target.

238
00:19:06,020 --> 00:19:11,600
And slowly but surely, it was almost like the music from My Fair Lady.

239
00:19:11,760 --> 00:19:13,780
You know, I think I've got it, I've got it, I've got it.

240
00:19:13,781 --> 00:19:19,460
And then pretty soon, the last puff of the rocket engine and the spacecraft stopped

241
00:19:19,461 --> 00:19:24,560
and the fine alignment on the telescope showed that we were pointing at the sun.

242
00:19:24,720 --> 00:19:27,240
And so we said, hey, we're okay.

243
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:32,720
Two days, 13 hours, and 29 minutes into the mission,

244
00:19:33,420 --> 00:19:38,640
the crew fires the lunar module descent engine to begin the free return flight path.

245
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:46,080
Running the ship on reduced power means the living conditions are difficult.

246
00:19:46,081 --> 00:19:57,120
The temperature inside the lunar module, or LEM, is dropping and soon reaches 38 degrees Fahrenheit, about as cold as a refrigerator.

247
00:19:59,120 --> 00:20:05,500
Water was collecting everywhere, and on every electric connector, a big glob of water was building up.

248
00:20:05,820 --> 00:20:12,480
After a day, all three of us huddled in the LEM, which in a way, I guess, helped just mutual body warmth.

249
00:20:12,481 --> 00:20:20,380
As the astronauts shiver in space amid their damp and dripping consoles, the equipment

250
00:20:20,381 --> 00:20:25,080
aboard Apollo 13, ironically, is in danger of overheating.

251
00:20:25,081 --> 00:20:30,940
Normally, water mixed with a coolant called glycol is circulated through the spacecraft

252
00:20:30,941 --> 00:20:34,340
to keep the equipment functioning properly.

253
00:20:34,341 --> 00:20:40,300
But on Apollo 13, water is scarce because two of the three fuel cells that produce it

254
00:20:40,301 --> 00:20:42,820
are damaged beyond repair.

255
00:20:42,821 --> 00:20:47,260
Heat will build up quickly in the spacecraft's electrical systems.

256
00:20:47,261 --> 00:20:52,560
Mission Control's only solution is to shut down all but the most critical devices.

257
00:20:52,561 --> 00:20:57,320
Get the spacecraft powered down as rapidly as we can to a survival mode,

258
00:20:57,321 --> 00:21:00,760
save the energy in case we might need it downstream,

259
00:21:00,761 --> 00:21:05,420
and this is going to put us on a plateau where we can think.

260
00:21:05,421 --> 00:21:08,800
Though he keeps a cool head, Kranz desperately needs to know

261
00:21:08,801 --> 00:21:12,060
how long the electrical power in the spacecraft could last.

262
00:21:15,340 --> 00:21:18,720
The news from his engineers is not good.

263
00:21:20,660 --> 00:21:27,800
To make it back to Earth, the astronaut's lifeboat would have to consume only 15 amps of electricity per hour.

264
00:21:28,620 --> 00:21:34,000
But the lunar module designers calculate the minimum draw at 20 amps per hour.

265
00:21:34,001 --> 00:21:40,120
somehow Kranz and his team have to get the draw down by five amps

266
00:21:40,121 --> 00:21:44,460
to make it possible for the crew of Apollo 13 to get home

267
00:21:44,461 --> 00:21:49,900
while the launch controllers get out their slide rules

268
00:21:49,901 --> 00:21:52,020
and grapple with the problem around the clock

269
00:21:52,021 --> 00:21:56,000
Kranz knows that even that will not be enough

270
00:21:56,001 --> 00:22:02,040
although the mainframe systems used by NASA were state of the art in 1970

271
00:22:02,041 --> 00:22:06,800
By today's standards, they are cumbersome and agonizingly slow,

272
00:22:07,220 --> 00:22:10,540
with less computing power than today's average laptop.

273
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:14,440
You have very little flexibility in using these machines.

274
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:16,540
That's the bad news.

275
00:22:16,620 --> 00:22:19,640
But the good news was it was a massive leap forward

276
00:22:19,641 --> 00:22:22,160
from the very primitive systems that we used

277
00:22:22,161 --> 00:22:26,020
in the early Mercury and the Gemini systems.

278
00:22:27,520 --> 00:22:31,740
The astronauts of Apollo 13 have occupied their wounded ship

279
00:22:31,741 --> 00:22:33,240
for three days.

280
00:22:33,241 --> 00:22:35,740
As the computers run the numbers,

281
00:22:35,741 --> 00:22:38,540
Kranz's team runs on pure adrenaline,

282
00:22:38,541 --> 00:22:40,540
surviving on catnaps.

283
00:22:40,541 --> 00:22:42,240
Working equations,

284
00:22:42,241 --> 00:22:45,240
trying to reconstruct why the explosion occurred

285
00:22:45,241 --> 00:22:47,240
and what can be done to fix it

286
00:22:47,241 --> 00:22:49,240
before it's too late.

287
00:22:52,240 --> 00:22:57,740
The Apollo Guidance computer is approximately the same size as today's desktop computer,

288
00:22:57,741 --> 00:23:01,240
but had as much as a hundred times less computing capacity.

289
00:23:01,241 --> 00:23:06,240
Apollo 13 will continue on Modern Marvels.

290
00:23:06,241 --> 00:23:11,240
We now return to Apollo 13 on Modern Marvels.

291
00:23:11,241 --> 00:23:18,240
At the time of the Apollo 13 mission, many Americans have become somewhat complacent about space flight.

292
00:23:18,241 --> 00:23:24,240
But with word of trouble in space, the mission becomes big news.

293
00:23:24,241 --> 00:23:28,240
People everywhere are glued to their televisions for the latest bulletin.

294
00:23:28,241 --> 00:23:36,520
latest bulletin. Newspapers carry banner headlines. Never before has a NASA failure

295
00:23:36,521 --> 00:23:42,259
been paraded around the world so nakedly and for so long.

296
00:23:42,260 --> 00:23:45,280
Space flight is also difficult

297
00:23:45,281 --> 00:23:49,952
for those left behind, the astronauts' loved ones and families.

298
00:23:49,953 --> 00:23:51,880
Surviving on whatever news

299
00:23:51,881 --> 00:23:57,100
NASA lets out, they want to know if their husbands, fathers and friends are all right.

300
00:23:57,101 --> 00:24:00,520
and what their chances are of making it back alive.

301
00:24:01,960 --> 00:24:04,480
Marilyn Lovell, wife of Commander James Lovell,

302
00:24:04,600 --> 00:24:07,580
must contend with hordes of reporters knocking on her door

303
00:24:07,581 --> 00:24:10,260
with questions that she can't answer.

304
00:24:12,260 --> 00:24:14,440
Are they going to get home? Are they going to hit the earth?

305
00:24:14,520 --> 00:24:16,660
Are they going to miss the earth? Are they getting poisoned?

306
00:24:16,661 --> 00:24:18,880
Are they running out of oxygen?

307
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:22,040
All these things sort of grow on the families.

308
00:24:22,041 --> 00:24:31,660
It's a much worse situation for those on the ground with their expectations of hearing that you've made it through,

309
00:24:32,340 --> 00:24:36,260
because literally they are helpless to be able to do anything during that period.

310
00:24:38,160 --> 00:24:44,400
In space, the men of Apollo 13 could only watch the blue marble of the Earth drift away,

311
00:24:44,940 --> 00:24:50,580
and imagine what might have been had they made their mark on the gray mystery drifting by below them.

312
00:24:50,581 --> 00:24:53,040
like the others who were there first.

313
00:24:54,420 --> 00:24:58,200
During Apollo 13's silent transit around the backside of the moon,

314
00:24:58,900 --> 00:25:01,920
the astronauts are out of contact with Earth tracking stations

315
00:25:01,921 --> 00:25:04,980
for 24 minutes and 35 seconds.

316
00:25:06,060 --> 00:25:07,460
Emerging on the other side,

317
00:25:07,960 --> 00:25:11,280
lunar gravitational force redirects their trajectory homeward.

318
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:17,920
Mr. Trance, can you tell us what sort of physical discomfort

319
00:25:17,921 --> 00:25:19,520
the crew is in at the moment?

320
00:25:20,580 --> 00:25:24,120
We're trying to work up some kind of a crew rest cycle right now

321
00:25:24,121 --> 00:25:26,180
to give them an adequate amount of rest

322
00:25:26,181 --> 00:25:29,260
between now and the possible mid-course correction at 104 hours.

323
00:25:30,180 --> 00:25:32,520
While those on the ground watch and worry,

324
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,620
they cannot guess how the cold and damp conditions

325
00:25:35,621 --> 00:25:38,580
inside the lunar module take their toll on the crew.

326
00:25:40,440 --> 00:25:42,020
Fred Hayes is the first victim,

327
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:44,200
falling ill with a bladder infection.

328
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,940
I did realize that at first he tried to keep it quiet for a while,

329
00:25:47,941 --> 00:25:54,080
But pretty soon, his teeth started chattering, and he was getting quite sick.

330
00:25:55,020 --> 00:25:59,900
Probably caused by the climate, severe cold, getting tired.

331
00:26:00,860 --> 00:26:02,580
We didn't drink enough water.

332
00:26:03,160 --> 00:26:08,920
But that combination of things allowed this bug to pick me out as the weakest one of the three.

333
00:26:09,820 --> 00:26:14,960
As Fred Hayes' condition gets worse, there was another crisis growing in intensity.

334
00:26:14,961 --> 00:26:21,960
The Apollo 13 crew was at risk of poisoning themselves with every breath.

335
00:26:21,961 --> 00:26:27,960
One of the things that had to be contended with was the carbon dioxide buildup in the

336
00:26:27,961 --> 00:26:32,960
space carrier from the crew expiration of their breath.

337
00:26:32,961 --> 00:26:36,960
And yes, it was anticipated to become a problem.

338
00:26:36,961 --> 00:26:42,960
With each breath in, the crew draws precious oxygen into their lungs.

339
00:26:42,961 --> 00:26:49,920
With each breath out, they expel carbon dioxide, a deadly substance in sufficient quantity.

340
00:26:52,480 --> 00:26:57,920
Normally, the waste carbon dioxide is removed by passing it through lithium hydroxide crystals

341
00:26:58,480 --> 00:27:00,812
packed in cylindrical canisters.

342
00:27:00,813 --> 00:27:05,840
But the onboard supply of those canisters is running dangerously low.

343
00:27:05,841 --> 00:27:13,720
A group of engineers recognized that we would eventually run out of the cylindrical canisters

344
00:27:13,721 --> 00:27:17,520
that we used to clean the carbon dioxide out of the air.

345
00:27:17,521 --> 00:27:21,860
But they also recognized we had plenty of square canisters over in the command and service

346
00:27:21,861 --> 00:27:24,820
module that weren't going to get used.

347
00:27:24,821 --> 00:27:27,880
We literally had square pegs and round holes.

348
00:27:27,881 --> 00:27:32,360
The different contractor built the system in the command module, used little cubical

349
00:27:32,361 --> 00:27:37,080
lithium hydroxide canisters and because of design considerations in the lunar module,

350
00:27:37,081 --> 00:27:39,740
they made them cylindrical.

351
00:27:39,741 --> 00:27:43,680
So they came up with the idea of fabricating an adapter

352
00:27:43,681 --> 00:27:49,120
made out of the cardboard cover of the flight plan, the plastic bag that they had their

353
00:27:49,121 --> 00:27:50,480
lunar suits in.

354
00:27:50,481 --> 00:27:54,660
We used a sock as a filter and then some duct tape and a hose

355
00:27:54,661 --> 00:27:58,540
and we fashioned this adapter and tested it in the laboratory.

356
00:27:58,541 --> 00:28:03,580
And then the engineers actually brought it in mission control so we could see what it looked like.

357
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:08,120
Then we wrote the procedures, now that we knew what it looked like,

358
00:28:08,200 --> 00:28:11,400
and how they told the crew how to make this device.

359
00:28:13,520 --> 00:28:16,920
Shaking their heads with amusement and using a lot of duct tape,

360
00:28:17,620 --> 00:28:23,480
the crew constructs a makeshift air purifier with the odd assortment of parts dictated by mission control.

361
00:28:24,500 --> 00:28:25,400
But it worked perfectly.

362
00:28:25,401 --> 00:28:31,040
As soon as we had it in place and had the air flowing through this jury-rigged canister,

363
00:28:31,420 --> 00:28:35,320
we saw that the partial pressure of carbon dioxide started to decrease.

364
00:28:35,980 --> 00:28:39,480
The jury-rigged adapter buys the crew more time,

365
00:28:40,220 --> 00:28:42,820
yet their most difficult challenges lay ahead.

366
00:28:44,720 --> 00:28:51,780
As their cold, dark spacecraft rapidly approaches Earth at 7,212 miles per hour,

367
00:28:51,781 --> 00:28:53,740
back on the ground.

368
00:28:54,440 --> 00:28:57,000
Flight controllers are laboring to come up with a way

369
00:28:57,001 --> 00:28:59,780
to bring the ship's electrical systems back online

370
00:28:59,781 --> 00:29:03,320
and bring Apollo 13 back to life

371
00:29:03,321 --> 00:29:06,540
without causing irreparable damage to the lifeboat.

372
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:08,800
The cruise only hope.

373
00:29:10,840 --> 00:29:14,700
The electrical system aboard Apollo 13's command module

374
00:29:14,701 --> 00:29:16,840
contains about 15 miles of wire,

375
00:29:17,380 --> 00:29:20,020
enough to wire 50 two-bedroom homes.

376
00:29:20,021 --> 00:29:23,720
Apollo 13 will return on Modern Marvels.

377
00:29:24,880 --> 00:29:29,060
We now return to Apollo 13 on Modern Marvels.

378
00:29:30,540 --> 00:29:35,260
As the astronauts of Apollo 13 drift toward home in their wounded spacecraft,

379
00:29:35,880 --> 00:29:38,640
they have done everything possible to conserve their resources.

380
00:29:39,660 --> 00:29:42,280
A free return course around the back of the moon

381
00:29:42,281 --> 00:29:47,320
has successfully used lunar gravity to slingshot Apollo 13 toward Earth

382
00:29:47,321 --> 00:29:50,300
with minimal course adjustments and fuel expenditure.

383
00:29:52,180 --> 00:29:55,480
But the largest problem of all remains to be solved.

384
00:29:56,680 --> 00:30:00,620
Does Apollo 13 have enough electrical power to get back to Earth?

385
00:30:02,260 --> 00:30:04,840
Gene Kranz, the flight director overseeing the mission,

386
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:07,500
has his team on a round-the-clock quest

387
00:30:07,501 --> 00:30:13,660
to calculate how the Apollo 13 spacecraft might draw no more than 15 amps per hour.

388
00:30:13,661 --> 00:30:21,020
Frankly, you just work whatever it takes to get the job done.

389
00:30:21,160 --> 00:30:27,340
To my recollection, I do not recall any arrests for any member of my team for almost two days.

390
00:30:29,940 --> 00:30:32,420
Answers were coming in from all over the country.

391
00:30:33,100 --> 00:30:35,360
A brain trust assembled by NASA.

392
00:30:36,060 --> 00:30:42,140
It included just about everyone who had built, worked on, or thought about the design of Apollo 13.

393
00:30:43,660 --> 00:30:47,780
including astronaut Ken Mattingly, who had been scrubbed from the mission.

394
00:30:48,260 --> 00:30:50,120
Mattingly was more or less the troubleshooter,

395
00:30:50,240 --> 00:30:54,860
moving between the various teams, looking at what was happening, commenting, critiquing.

396
00:30:54,861 --> 00:31:00,060
Ken is the kind of guy you like to have on the team because he asks the right questions.

397
00:31:00,880 --> 00:31:07,780
And he also senses options and alternatives that maybe people really weren't developing.

398
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:12,580
Kranz and his team are faced with the problem of writing a procedure

399
00:31:12,581 --> 00:31:15,080
for something that has never been attempted.

400
00:31:17,400 --> 00:31:20,380
Before Apollo 13 can begin re-entry,

401
00:31:21,140 --> 00:31:24,220
the spacecraft systems have to be brought back online.

402
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:27,620
It is a tall order.

403
00:31:28,480 --> 00:31:33,080
Powered down, Apollo 13 is transmitting scant data back to Houston.

404
00:31:34,520 --> 00:31:40,140
In order to extend the consumables so we could coast back to the Earth,

405
00:31:40,141 --> 00:31:42,040
which is going to take another 100 and some odd hours,

406
00:31:42,580 --> 00:31:46,480
They had to take all the electrical power possible off the lunar module.

407
00:31:47,320 --> 00:31:50,240
And so that meant just turning off all of the guidance computers.

408
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:55,980
We left ourselves with a fan to circulate the cabin atmosphere, a radio to talk.

409
00:31:56,780 --> 00:31:57,560
And that's about it.

410
00:31:57,920 --> 00:32:02,380
The most disturbing thing to the folks on the ground was the fact that all this equipment turned off.

411
00:32:03,100 --> 00:32:06,160
They had no further insight into the health of the spacecraft.

412
00:32:06,161 --> 00:32:13,040
Without active instrumentation, the launch controllers have to find a way to power up

413
00:32:13,041 --> 00:32:20,000
the spacecraft systems with the least amount of electrical drain.

414
00:32:20,001 --> 00:32:24,820
Since the lunar module is still connected to the command module, engineers pursue the

415
00:32:24,821 --> 00:32:28,000
most logical path.

416
00:32:28,001 --> 00:32:31,400
Allow the two units to share electrical resources.

417
00:32:31,401 --> 00:32:38,000
It was a long, complex procedure which took people all over the country, contributing

418
00:32:38,001 --> 00:32:44,840
to just a running series of debates and discussions about how to do it, until they finally came

419
00:32:44,841 --> 00:32:50,600
up with a plan which seemed to fit the power budget.

420
00:32:50,601 --> 00:32:54,480
At this point, the lunar module is close to freezing.

421
00:32:54,481 --> 00:32:59,480
The walls and windows drip with condensed water.

422
00:32:59,481 --> 00:33:07,480
While the ground team labors over the plan, aboard Apollo 13, Commander James Lovell is

423
00:33:07,481 --> 00:33:09,480
preoccupied with one inescapable fact.

424
00:33:09,481 --> 00:33:14,480
The blue-green orb of the Earth is getting closer, and he hasn't received instructions

425
00:33:14,481 --> 00:33:19,480
from Mission Control on exactly how he will restart his spacecraft's electrical systems.

426
00:33:19,481 --> 00:33:24,960
They were very slow in getting those instructions up, and I was getting very worried.

427
00:33:25,100 --> 00:33:28,240
I mean, we were all in, of course, that it was going to intercept the earth in some manner.

428
00:33:28,760 --> 00:33:31,600
And I knew that our people were getting very, very tired.

429
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:35,520
And I tried to chastise the control center saying,

430
00:33:35,660 --> 00:33:39,740
let's just get the stuff to turn this thing on and make sure we get it back home safely.

431
00:33:41,320 --> 00:33:44,780
The procedure is so complex that when finally complete,

432
00:33:44,781 --> 00:33:49,380
it encompasses 400 entries and is 39 pages long.

433
00:33:49,480 --> 00:33:56,600
As Lovell pilots the spacecraft, Swigert, with assistance from Hayes, copies down the procedure by hand.

434
00:33:57,380 --> 00:33:59,163
No mistakes can be tolerated.

435
00:33:59,164 --> 00:34:03,160
A flawless execution is required for the crew to come back alive.

436
00:34:03,900 --> 00:34:12,300
We were trying to voice up the procedures to the crew for the final entry that we realized how this exhaustion,

437
00:34:12,301 --> 00:34:18,780
the cold temperatures, and the dehydration that Fred Hayes was suffering.

438
00:34:18,781 --> 00:34:24,060
and yet he was the man who had to execute the procedures on board the lunar module.

439
00:34:25,140 --> 00:34:28,900
It was read up to us and we did not have blank paper.

440
00:34:29,940 --> 00:34:37,700
So we had to use the inside and back and front covers on checklist books and burn cards

441
00:34:37,701 --> 00:34:41,120
that were meant to be filled in for burns, but we used those

442
00:34:41,121 --> 00:34:44,380
because it was very lengthy and it all had to be handwritten.

443
00:34:44,381 --> 00:34:49,800
For the power-up procedure to be successful, each man has to do his part.

444
00:34:50,500 --> 00:34:56,800
With Lovell guiding the ship, Hayes in the LEM, and Swigert in the command module,

445
00:34:57,000 --> 00:35:00,640
they all have to flip a dizzying array of switches in just the right order.

446
00:35:01,420 --> 00:35:04,240
We had the lunar module that we were using as our lifeboat.

447
00:35:04,780 --> 00:35:09,760
We had the cone-shaped command module, which was required for reentry.

448
00:35:09,860 --> 00:35:13,080
It had the only heat shield, but it had a very limited electrical power lifetime.

449
00:35:13,081 --> 00:35:15,380
Then we had the damaged service module.

450
00:35:15,580 --> 00:35:20,180
We had to maneuver this stock of spacecraft into an orientation

451
00:35:20,181 --> 00:35:23,740
so we could separate the pieces on different trajectories.

452
00:35:24,620 --> 00:35:29,680
The men of Apollo 13 have passed five days and seven hours in space.

453
00:35:30,300 --> 00:35:35,080
It is up to this exhausted crew, who have been subsisting on three hours of sleep per night,

454
00:35:35,520 --> 00:35:38,500
to safely guide their spacecraft into the recovery zone.

455
00:35:38,501 --> 00:35:45,500
We have to come inside a two degree pie-shaped wedge that comes down to the atmosphere.

456
00:35:45,501 --> 00:35:49,500
No less than five and a half degrees, no greater than seven and a half degrees.

457
00:35:49,501 --> 00:35:52,500
We had to thread ourselves inside that atmosphere for a safe landing.

458
00:35:52,501 --> 00:35:56,500
If we come in too shallow, we skip out like skipping a stone on water.

459
00:35:56,501 --> 00:36:02,500
If we come in too steep, the sudden deceleration makes us a fiery meteor for a few brief seconds, and that would be it.

460
00:36:02,501 --> 00:36:06,500
The timeline is getting tighter.

461
00:36:06,501 --> 00:36:12,400
The crew's recovery window is small, and as it happens, nature is not on their side.

462
00:36:12,401 --> 00:36:19,900
A typhoon is developing in the recovery area, making the odds even slimmer for the safe return of Apollo 13.

463
00:36:19,901 --> 00:36:28,300
When an Apollo spacecraft re-entered Earth's atmosphere, it generated enough energy to light up Los Angeles for a minute and a half.

464
00:36:28,301 --> 00:36:32,800
Apollo 13 will return on Modern Marvels.

465
00:36:32,801 --> 00:36:37,620
We now return to Apollo 13 on Modern Marvels.

466
00:36:39,740 --> 00:36:46,660
The entire Apollo 13 mission hangs in the balance as the crew begins the complex power-up procedure.

467
00:36:47,620 --> 00:36:50,440
The job is expected to take 30 minutes to complete.

468
00:36:54,240 --> 00:37:02,600
Even though the spacecraft is chilly and damp, thankfully, not one electrical circuit shorts out from condensation on the wires.

469
00:37:02,800 --> 00:37:10,800
On the ground, mission control watches as the spacecraft systems come back online.

470
00:37:10,801 --> 00:37:16,720
Command module pilot Jack Swigert is as tired as the rest of the crew, but he faces an absolutely

471
00:37:16,721 --> 00:37:19,520
critical task.

472
00:37:19,521 --> 00:37:23,300
Jack had the critical thing of throwing the right switch so he got rid of the service

473
00:37:23,301 --> 00:37:26,640
module so he wouldn't also get rid of Fred and I on the lunar module.

474
00:37:26,641 --> 00:37:31,700
He taped a little thing on the lunar module jettison switch saying no,

475
00:37:32,380 --> 00:37:36,420
so that he wouldn't throw that one when Fred and I were in there.

476
00:37:37,800 --> 00:37:41,880
Swigert throws the right switch, and the service module is detached.

477
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:47,340
Despite their fatigue and Fred Hayes' high fever,

478
00:37:47,341 --> 00:37:51,180
the crew completes the entire procedure in just 21 minutes.

479
00:37:51,181 --> 00:38:02,060
the crew watches amazed as the service module drifts away in space 39 529 miles from earth

480
00:38:02,061 --> 00:38:09,720
and there's one whole side of that big business is that right right by the high

481
00:38:09,721 --> 00:38:16,140
gate antenna the whole panel is blown out almost from the uh base to the engine copy that

482
00:38:16,141 --> 00:38:25,320
While the lunar module is okay, for the first time it is clear how much damage to the service module has been caused by the explosion.

483
00:38:28,080 --> 00:38:31,440
Back at mission control, there is more bad news.

484
00:38:32,180 --> 00:38:35,060
The spacecraft is drawing two more amps than it ought to.

485
00:38:36,800 --> 00:38:43,640
Tolerances are so tight that Apollo 13 might run short of electrical power just when it's needed most.

486
00:38:43,641 --> 00:38:50,980
added to that concern is the knowledge that the spacecraft has drifted off course

487
00:38:50,981 --> 00:38:58,080
it was dicey all the way to the very end the navy came in and said we have a typhoon developing

488
00:38:58,081 --> 00:39:03,860
in the pacific just off samoa where we're bringing spacecraft back to and they were really

489
00:39:03,861 --> 00:39:09,960
looking at seeing if we could change our landing point any but that is when we determined that

490
00:39:09,961 --> 00:39:12,580
we're actually not on the proper return path.

491
00:39:13,140 --> 00:39:16,780
As we're now approaching the Earth's atmosphere, we're only about eight hours out.

492
00:39:17,460 --> 00:39:19,280
The trajectory people come in and say,

493
00:39:19,380 --> 00:39:22,680
hey, flight, we are off our return trajectory again.

494
00:39:22,740 --> 00:39:27,400
We have to perform another emergency maneuver to get back in the reentry corridor.

495
00:39:28,560 --> 00:39:30,940
The two extra amps are vanquished.

496
00:39:30,941 --> 00:39:33,140
When admission controls instruction,

497
00:39:33,640 --> 00:39:37,400
part of Apollo 13's backup navigation system is shut down.

498
00:39:39,960 --> 00:39:46,960
Finally, reentry begins after five difficult days in space.

499
00:39:46,961 --> 00:39:52,960
When you first hit the atmosphere, the very upper reaches, you're ionizing the air to some degree,

500
00:39:52,961 --> 00:39:56,960
so the spacecraft, including the interior, takes on a little white glow.

501
00:39:56,961 --> 00:40:00,960
Just think of yourself inside of a neon light bulb.

502
00:40:00,961 --> 00:40:05,960
The ionization of air around the command module during reentry

503
00:40:05,961 --> 00:40:10,700
prevents radio contact with mission control for a period of about four minutes.

504
00:40:11,340 --> 00:40:16,860
But during Apollo 13's re-entry, five minutes pass with no communication from the crew.

505
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:22,320
Mission control becomes absolutely silent.

506
00:40:22,500 --> 00:40:26,320
All you hear is the noise of the air conditioning and the hum of the electronics

507
00:40:26,321 --> 00:40:29,400
and every eye is on the wall, clocks counting down to zero.

508
00:40:30,200 --> 00:40:33,780
When it finally hits zero, you say, Apollo 13, Houston.

509
00:40:33,781 --> 00:40:35,920
and you don't get any response.

510
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:36,760
All you hear is static.

511
00:40:37,300 --> 00:40:39,180
And you call again and you call again

512
00:40:39,181 --> 00:40:40,740
and finally after one minute,

513
00:40:41,180 --> 00:40:43,320
for the first time in this mission,

514
00:40:43,560 --> 00:40:45,640
you're wondering what in the hell happened.

515
00:40:47,240 --> 00:40:50,380
But hope is eternal there.

516
00:40:50,460 --> 00:40:51,340
You keep calling

517
00:40:51,341 --> 00:40:53,420
and at one minute and 27 seconds,

518
00:40:53,520 --> 00:40:54,420
you got this call.

519
00:40:56,500 --> 00:40:57,420
Okay, go.

520
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:01,060
Okay, we read you, Jack.

521
00:41:01,061 --> 00:41:05,060
That was Command Module Pilot Jack Swigert.

522
00:41:05,061 --> 00:41:09,060
And when you see those parachutes blossom on that screen,

523
00:41:09,061 --> 00:41:13,060
I believe many of the mission controllers, myself included, literally cried.

524
00:41:13,061 --> 00:41:18,060
Five days, 22 hours, and 54 minutes into the mission,

525
00:41:18,061 --> 00:41:24,060
the Command Module splashes down within four miles of the recovery aircraft carrier.

526
00:41:24,061 --> 00:41:34,060
The splashdown comes and of course, if we bob back up again and we see water on the windshield,

527
00:41:34,061 --> 00:41:37,060
there's a great, a great sigh of relief.

528
00:41:37,061 --> 00:41:43,060
The weary and chilled astronauts board the recovery ship, USS Iwo Jima.

529
00:41:43,061 --> 00:41:51,060
The chaplain then turned and he gave a prayer of thanks for the millions and millions of

530
00:41:51,061 --> 00:41:53,060
people who prayed for our safe return.

531
00:41:53,061 --> 00:41:58,480
Oh Lord, we joyfully welcome back to Earth astronauts Lovell, Hayes, and Swigert.

532
00:41:59,020 --> 00:42:04,380
We offer our humble thanksgiving for this successful recovery. Amen.

533
00:42:05,220 --> 00:42:12,120
And when that crew hit the carrier deck, and when we handed over our responsibility to the aircraft carrier task force commander,

534
00:42:12,520 --> 00:42:20,200
then we could celebrate, and it was only then that we could feel this intense release that we had pulled off a miracle.

535
00:42:20,201 --> 00:42:27,260
When the astronauts are reunited with their families in Hawaii, President Richard Nixon

536
00:42:27,261 --> 00:42:31,420
awards them the Presidential Medal of Freedom.

537
00:42:31,421 --> 00:42:35,640
And the world rejoices at the crew's safe return.

538
00:42:35,641 --> 00:42:41,540
Well, it was sort of hard to grasp on all this positive attention we were getting.

539
00:42:41,541 --> 00:42:47,120
The one letter that I cherish, Charles Lindbergh, wrote me that he was in the Philippines,

540
00:42:47,121 --> 00:42:50,500
been listening to the radio from Manila to how far we were coming and whether we were

541
00:42:50,501 --> 00:42:53,440
going to make it or not.

542
00:42:53,441 --> 00:42:59,320
Lindbergh, like the astronauts of Apollo 13, knew about staring down fear and overcoming

543
00:42:59,321 --> 00:43:03,060
the limitations of a flying machine.

544
00:43:03,061 --> 00:43:07,000
Apollo 13, mission-wise, was a failure.

545
00:43:07,001 --> 00:43:15,520
But it was a triumph in human ability to take a crisis and through crisis management get

546
00:43:15,521 --> 00:43:18,820
almost certain catastrophe back home safely.

547
00:43:20,940 --> 00:43:23,860
A triumph of ingenuity and guts,

548
00:43:24,480 --> 00:43:26,920
the Apollo 13 mission is often called

549
00:43:26,921 --> 00:43:29,200
NASA's most successful failure.

550
00:43:33,260 --> 00:43:37,400
It caused the American Space Agency to re-examine itself

551
00:43:37,401 --> 00:43:39,560
and the purpose of the space program.

552
00:43:42,260 --> 00:43:44,260
In a mission post-mortem,

553
00:43:44,261 --> 00:43:49,540
engineers learned that excess voltage fed to the oxygen tank a month before

554
00:43:49,541 --> 00:43:56,680
launch had damaged a thermostat causing the tank to overheat and degrading the

555
00:43:56,681 --> 00:44:02,380
insulation on the electrical wiring when the fan was turned on for the cryo

556
00:44:02,381 --> 00:44:07,960
stir a short circuit resulted in a spark which caused the tank to catch fire and

557
00:44:07,961 --> 00:44:08,960
and explode.

558
00:44:12,800 --> 00:44:15,460
The lessons learned on the Apollo 13 mission

559
00:44:15,560 --> 00:44:17,460
will remain with NASA forever

560
00:44:18,460 --> 00:44:19,960
and bring a deeper meaning

561
00:44:20,260 --> 00:44:22,960
to the grand mission of going to the moon.

562
00:44:26,360 --> 00:44:28,780
For some, it has been seen as an act of necessity.

